# The stored people nearest each query, nearest first. The node serves this
# type twice:
#
# - match_faces searches face samples. Its candidates are the people kept
#   across sessions and the people met in the open session.
# - match_appearances searches only the appearance samples recorded in the
#   open session, because clothes change between sessions. With no session
#   open, it finds no one.
#
# Each person is in a query's list once, with the distance of their nearest
# sample (walkie-database ADR-0018). The node never decides whether a query
# is a stranger.

# At most 32. Each query is compared only with samples of its own model. An
# empty list succeeds, with no results.
ModelVector[] queries
# At most this many people per query. 0 means 10, and more than 100 means
# 100. Every list repeats the name and facts of each person in it, so ask
# for no more people than you need.
uint32 max_results
---
# False when the search could not run. That happens when a query names a
# model with no table of its kind, has the wrong number of values, is all
# zeros, or holds NaN or infinity, when there are more than 32 queries, or
# when the database failed. message then says why. When the database failed,
# it starts with "The database could not". A search that finds no one
# succeeds, with empty lists.
bool success
# For example "Matched 2 queries.". When max_results left people out of any
# list, it ends in "(more not shown)." instead.
string message
# One list per query, in the order of queries.
PersonMatches[] results
